### Positioning against 2610.02344 (cross-cutting, required for the keep/revise/add statement)

Not a fourth direction; it runs through A-C and costs little:
- P1 equation-level map of mu_i to the delivered quantities: **confirmed** by `idea-repo` in
  idea-repo#4 (brief v4.2 at 5f0fe33, Sec. 5.1; `refs/lit_dvd2610.md`). The paper states three
  qualifications:
  1. the headline count (Eq. 6-7) has no weight decay and is the window's upper edge only; it is
     reported next to the J.1 variant (`dvd_nowd` and `dvd_signed` in P2);
  2. masked targets and input-space masking are a transfer, worded as "their criterion applied
     with our masked moments";
  3. for random or non-commuting predictors their exact object is the unstable-eigenvalue count
     of Thm A.1, and r_tight bounds the rank of the *linearised* flow (Cor. 3.3), not the final
     rank. The exceedances measured in P2 (57% / 26%) are what predictor decay produces (modes
     that escape early die later, the race's point), and the paper says so rather than calling
     their bound wrong.

  For deep predictors the identification holds with P0 = the product of the factors. Their
  Prop. J.5 (mimetic init, integrated growth along a decaying identity dose) is the nearest thing
  to the race and is cited; it is neither gain decay nor a rank predictor. Their reading: the
  linearisation holds the predictor at its O(1) value; with a scalar predictor their escape
  condition with encoder weight decay (gamma_i - sigma_i > eta_wd, Prop. J.1) is the delivered
  g_k(p0) > 0, i.e. the **initial window** that E1b scored at rank MAE 3.7 vs 0.25 for the race
  and 11.2 for the static bound (1017 held-out runs); their headline count r_tight = #{mu > 1}
  (Eq. 7-8) omits weight decay, has no lower window edge and is called by them "an upper bound
  and not a prediction of ERank"; predictor weight decay never enters their theory. It does not
  treat AdamW vs SGD, schedules or the post-capture stage (beyond an adiabatic result, F.1), or
  deep/nonlinear encoders except "by analogy" for images, where the initial mu does not separate
  the predictors (K.1). **Consequence: directions A-C are not pre-empted and are kept unchanged.**
- P2 baselines from it in **every comparison** of E4-E8 (alongside the static bound, early
  reading and the step-0 rule). **Done on the delivered E1b/E2 runs** by `experiment-repo`
  (112f91e: `code/baselines/dvd_mu.py`, `results/baselines/dvd_mu.json`, per-run CSVs), with
  four variants: `dvd` (their main-text form), `dvd_signed` (their App. C.8; with the predictor
  fixed it equals the delivered initial window run by run), `dvd_sorted` (their Eq. 31) and
  `dvd_nowd` (mu > 1 without the weight-decay floor). Measured there (retrospective, since those
  runs were pre-registered for the race): the race has the lower rank error on every linear and
  masking arm (e.g. E1b grid 0.25 vs 3.74; E2 cyclic 0.03 vs 1.04). With their paired Eq. 51/Eq. 7
  predictor side and the exact linearisation added (409a766), the race still has the lower error
  on the random-predictor arm (0.69 vs 3.02 for their best variant) and the non-commuting arm
  (0.11 vs 1.28). Their count, stated as a bound, exceeds the measured rank in 57% of aligned E1b
  runs and 26% of E2 cyclic runs. **Their signed form beats the race on the E2 MLP arm (1.90 vs
  2.69)**, and both fail on the tanh encoder. The MLP loss is
  a located failure the paper reports and C rung 2 carries (L2 stays open). Variant rule: every
  variant is reported; none is chosen as "theirs" after outcomes. For new grids the registered
  baselines are their published readout (`dvd`, r_tight from the published mu summary) and
  `dvd_signed`/initial window, fixed in each `prediction.json`;
- P3 a head-to-head in **their** setting (code pinned at commit 3176215b, environment recorded;
  torch 2.5.1 is inside their range), in three parts kept distinct:
  (r) **retrospective, zero compute, never cut:** race, `dvd`, `dvd_signed` and mu-count scored
  from inputs alone on their public outcomes (`results/tabular_phase_grid/full_sweep_per_run.csv`,
  810 rows, in their repository) and on their jannis predictor-scale pattern (Tab. 16: only
  alpha = 0.05 learns; alpha = 0.01 collapses in 4/5 contexts despite mu_50 > 1). Both are
  labelled retrospective. On their grid the decay is tiny (lr x wd <= 1e-8, the lambda_P -> 0
  limit), so race = window there is an **implementation consistency check, not a test**, and a
  tie is not a win;
  (a) **prospective cells off their grid**, at weight decays where race and window differ, and
  (b) new seeds/cells of the predictor-scale pattern. A pre-registered test exists only for runs
  executed after the `prediction.json` is committed. Before any run it registers: the cell list,
  chosen from inputs alone; the minimum predicted difference in surviving directions at which
  race and window count as differing (coincident cells are reported as ties); the minimum number
  of differing cells. If the pilot yields fewer, the extension is stated as non-discriminating,
  not enlarged. 3 new seeds per cell;
  **metric:** one primary statistic for both criteria on the same cells, the rank MAE of the
  thresholded count (>= 1e-3 x max, as in `plan_v1.md`) with Spearman and 95% CI. Their ERank
  (exp-entropy of normalised singular values) is reported alongside through a registered
  count-to-ERank map;
  **cost** is unmeasured, so a single cell is timed first (jannis, 40 epochs, wandb disabled) on
  local-compute at the loaded rate (<= 1 CPU-h). E10 is sized to <= 10 GPU-h-equivalent and
  <= ~400 runs in total; a run over ~10 CPU-min goes to Ibex under the v3 job rules;
- P4 **ResidualPred** as a row of C rung 5 (re-scoped in v2.8; cut with E10 (a)/(b)).
  - The image ResidualPred (brief v4.2 Sec. 5.1, `ResidualPredPredictor`) has three parts:
    - a learnable per-head diagonal bias, beta_init = 10, on the context-context attention block
      only;
    - a zero-initialised, undecayed MLP fc2;
    - a final LayerNorm before `proj`.
  - Its end-to-end gain is therefore **not** about 1: their K.1 measures about 0.31 for both
    blocks. The identity acts as a token-routing prior (their App. E.3), outside both linear
    theories.
  - In the delivered theory a scalar gain above the window makes directions shrink first, so
    neither theory predicts a gain-based rank benefit.
  - P4 asks whether the race, computed from the measured gain trajectory exactly as in C5,
    accounts for ResidualPred's rank relative to the standard predictor.
    - If yes, the effect is gain-mediated.
    - If the race predicts no difference and the rank differs, the benefit lies outside the
      linear theories. It is reported as a located limit of both, not explained away.
  - Their frozen ResidualPred collapses (I.8), as the delivered E1 result predicts.
  - Implementation: their `image/` code is CC BY-NC 4.0, so it is **reimplemented** in the
    delivered ViT. The delivered code lists its differences from the original and carries none
    of their repository's author-identifying text (anonymity).
  - Gain: an operational "predictor gain over time" is registered before the instrumented run,
    e.g. the top singular values of the predictor's feature-space map on a fixed batch, as in
    their K.1. The initial readout of the reimplementation is checked against their about 0.31,
    which tests the reimplementation, not either theory.
  - The prediction (time and set of surviving directions) is committed first. Either outcome is
    reported.
  - If the reimplementation does not reproduce their higher rank at our recipe, that is
    reported as a reimplementation outcome, not as a test of either theory.
- The paper credits the drive-vs-decay framing and the heuristic unification to 2610.02344
  (prior work under the cutoff) and states each claim's novelty relative to it; any predictor
  design the extension recommends is compared with ResidualPred (P4) or the paper says why not.
- Owners: P1 `idea-repo`; P2-P4 `experiment-repo`; the positioning text `paper-repo` (the
  framing, the linearisation at the collapsed point with an O(1) predictor, "heuristics shift the
  per-mode ratio" and masking as Sigma_X1 = p Sigma_XX are credited, as are EMA not changing
  membership, the frozen identity predictor collapsing (J.6, I.8) and "large encoder weight decay
  stabilises collapse" (J.1, not novel on its own); still distinct, wording to be
  checked against their Eq. 7-8 and App. J: race/capture and the separatrix, the decaying
  predictor gain and the necessity of predictor decay, lambda^race far below the static threshold,
  the two-sided window and rank non-monotone in p0 and lambda, the pre-registered held-out tests).

